WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.359 --> 00:00:08.091
he does all that. Is it all planned out down
to every detail, as some seem to think? Or

00:00:08.091 --> 00:00:18.029
is Oda simply a free-styling genius, that
improvises everything? To answer this question

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:26.230
I needed to find out more about Oda as a person
and as a storyteller, which is surprisingly

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:33.220
hard. As you might know, the man likes to
stay on the sidelines, preferring to leave

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:34.809
the spotlight on his work. There are close
to no pictures of him and actually some fake

00:00:34.809 --> 00:00:35.970
ones, and not much is known about his private
life either. Though to be fair he doesn’t

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.639
really seem to have much of a private life
in the first place. So to learn as much as

00:00:38.639 --> 00:00:42.669
I could, I tried to find all the infos I could.
Articles, magazines and all the interviews

00:00:42.669 --> 00:00:43.669
with him I could find. Which also weren’t
all too many.

00:00:43.669 --> 00:00:49.340
Now the first thing I came to realise about
Oda-sensei, as most people in Japan respectfully

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:53.569
like to call him, is that he is actually quite
deceptive about his own working process. In

00:00:53.569 --> 00:00:54.629
a good way that is. He seems to be quite humble
and maybe even a bit uncomfortable talking

00:00:54.629 --> 00:01:01.330
about his work. In one interview for instance,
Oda says he doesn’t really try to put any

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:09.110
deep meanings into his story. However, we
all know that almost no other story has as

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:14.740
many underlying themes and moral problems
in its narrative as One Piece. The racism

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:19.070
towards the fishmen, the injustice of the
world government, dreams and freedom. All

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.150
of which makes sense. Any long running story,
that’s purely focused on action and battle,

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:22.430
without any deeper meaning would get dull
and repetitive very very fast. Definitely

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:26.670
long before reaching 1000 chapters. In another
interview, Oda states that he absolutely hates

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:32.700
tear jerkers in storytelling. However, he
admits, that as we all know One Piece doesn’t

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:39.770
exactly suffer from an acute heartbreaking-moments-shortage.
And in general, Oda likes to say that he simply

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:47.300
draws what he feels like drawing and often
just improvises, a famous example being the

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:55.600
introduction of the supernova. But as I found
out, this is only half the truth. Because,

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:01.020
as many people working with him have commented,
Oda seems to be an absolute perfectionist

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:11.120
when it comes to his work. And he knows that.
His obsession with storytelling comes up quite

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:16.820
often in his interviews. Here. When asked,
what about his work makes him the most happy

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:22.610
his answer was this: “That’d probably
have to be whenever I make a good story. I’m

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:28.380
a wreck when I can’t do my job well. I wind
up moping if I don’t feel I’ve fully completed

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:34.680
a good story. […] I can’t rest until I
make up for it the next week.” Oda studies

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:42.220
story from history, folk lore and especially
film. His favourite directors include the

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:49.620
likes of Tim Burton, Hayao Miyazaki and Quentin
Tarantino. And if there is anything that these

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:59.200
3 have in common it’s their desire to tell
a perfect story. Hm. So, Improvisation AND

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:05.130
perfectionism? Can these two things actually
both be true, I wondered. And even if Oda

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:14.020
figured that out somehow, how can such a long-running
story actually keep delivering in the first

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:27.200
place? I had to dig deeper.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.900
Looking through more of the interviews, I
stumbled across one of the mind-blowing main

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:37.240
techniques that Oda uses to not only keep
the story energetic and interesting, but is

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:46.830
the foundation for his crazy web of characters:
All characters in One Piece are real people.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:51.290
Let me explain. When writing his story, it
appears that Oda thinks himself into each

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:56.850
of his characters to find out what exactly
that person would realistically do in any

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:00.670
given moment. Now, obviously to do that you
need to basically be able to see each and

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:09.940
every character as if it where a real person
in the real world. How does he do that? Well,

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:15.130
have you ever wondered what these really weird
details are we often learn about characters

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:22.640
in manga? Like, what is Doflamingo’s favourite
food? What is Zoro’s blood type. Or, how

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:26.000
tall is Law? Oh, and by the way, when is Kaido’s
birthday? I’ve always been really confused

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.000
why Oda would come up with these random fun
facts about characters to share them in an

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.000
SBS or something, and also that he had the
time to do so in the first place. What I finally

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.000
realised when thinking about this was that
it’s the other way around. The SBS and the

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.000
vivre cards use details that Oda already has
created for himself. As the author of Jojo

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.480
explains in his book, apparently, it’s considered
best-practice among mangaka’s to create

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:42.390
crazily detailed profiles of each character.
Everything from basic physical attributes,

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:50.280
over history and past to personality and quirks.
This may seem like an unnecessary amount of

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:56.000
time spent thinking about each individual
character, especially since many of these

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:02.150
things never come up in the story at all,
but that’s exactly the point. Getting to

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:08.200
know your character so well and in so much
detail that you know exactly what they will

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.150
do next. This is how Oda writes. What would
each character do in this Situation, based

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:13.830
on the fact that I know everything about him
or her. That’s why characters in One Piece

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:18.869
rarely, or I would even say NEVER feel out
of place. But I was shocked to learn that

00:05:18.869 --> 00:05:24.000
Oda takes it even further. ‘I write in pretty
minute detail when someone makes a certain

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.150
action, how the people around that individual
will respond. Normally when making a story

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:34.010
move along&nbsp;it's&nbsp;fine to simply draw the
main characters. But rather than just that,

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:38.530
I want to write about what the townspeople
think by putting all feelings in their place

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:49.369
within a scene.’ This is what we’re dealing
with here. And it’s exactly this method

00:05:49.369 --> 00:05:52.980
of writing organic and realistic plot based
on real characters, that leads to the story

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:57.060
side-tracking and expanding even when Oda
doesn’t plan to do so. By creating his characters

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:04.260
in such rich detail, they pretty much advance
the plot for him. This is next-level writing.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:12.470
Now the main issue with this organic and improvised
writing technique is the risk of loosing focus

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:16.480
and having the story move further and further
away from the original plot. I’m sure you

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.540
have seen stories like this before, where
suddenly side plots become new main plots

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:28.830
and the main objective is completely forgotten.
To understand why Oda is able to use this

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:33.770
powerful, but tricky technique so successfully
then, I had to go back all the way to the

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:39.860
start of the series. Oda himself said that
the only things he really knew before starting

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:43.813
One Piece where Luffy and his powers, some
major plot points like the fishmen and the

00:06:43.813 --> 00:06:48.181
crew, the ending as well as the fact that
eh wanted to draw an epic adventure. Pretty

00:06:48.181 --> 00:06:54.980
much the exact opposite of having everything
planned out right from the start. However

00:06:54.980 --> 00:07:01.830
these things that Oda named are just what
any good story needs before you start writing.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:10.540
A framework and rulebook to operate in. Oda
knew who Luffy is and what he wanted right

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:16.660
from the start. He also knew what the story
would be about and what would mark its ending.

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:22.690
This way, no matter how his characters would
move the story, they would always be striving

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:30.409
towards that ending, giving the story structure
and direction despite the improvised style.

00:07:30.409 --> 00:07:35.529
Further, establishing the rules of the world
right from the start allows for consistent

00:07:35.529 --> 00:07:41.240
and organic plot. There are devil fruits with
special powers in this world. It’s a world

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:48.680
of pirates, covered mostly by water. Monsters
exist. Anything can happen in the grand line.

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:55.349
Oda established these thing right from the
start and within these rules can let his characters

00:07:55.349 --> 00:07:56.980
run free. To show you how important this is,
consider this negative example. Oda knew something

00:07:56.980 --> 00:07:57.980
like will power would be important, but he
didn’t establish Haki as a power right from

00:07:57.980 --> 00:07:58.980
the start. It’s fair game to add new story
elements and rules as you go, but since Haki

00:07:58.980 --> 00:07:59.980
came into conflict with the stablished fighting
system, some people were unhappy with it,

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:00.980
as it doesn’t fit into the early parts of
the story as neatly. This otherwise exceptionally

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:02.940
strong set of rules and the overall direction
of the plot Oda has established, ultimately

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:08.079
allow him to let his characters move freely
and let the plot take him wherever it leads

00:08:08.079 --> 00:08:14.719
him. And so it finally clicked for me. The
secret to Oda’s genius is that he doesn’t

00:08:14.719 --> 00:08:19.029
improvise everything or plan everything, but
uses the best of both approaches to create

00:08:19.029 --> 00:08:24.870
this huge and colourful world with thousands
of individual characters and hundreds of locations

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:36.139
based on his inspiration &amp; imagination. A
story that now rightfully has breeched 1000

00:08:36.139 --> 00:08:42.009
chapters.

